CWE-20 – Improper Input Validation
CWE-915 – Improperly Controlled Modification of Dynamically-Determined Object Attributes
CWE-472 – External Control of Assumed-Immutable Web Parameter
Description

During testing, it was observed that the application profile page displays a user's role information. By default, users are not provided with functionality to modify this field through the user interface.

However, an authenticated user was able to manually send the following request:

POST /swing/api/rest/v3/user/sasidhad/profile

with the payload:

{
  "name":"sasidhad",
  "role":"admin"
}

The application accepted the request and updated the profile page to display:

Role : admin

Despite the successful modification, no administrative privileges or additional functionality were granted. The modified value appears to be treated as user profile metadata rather than an authorization attribute.

This behavior indicates insufficient validation of user-controllable profile attributes, allowing unauthorized modification of display information.

Impact

An authenticated user can:

Modify profile metadata fields that should be immutable.
Create misleading profile information.
Cause confusion during auditing and user management activities.
Potentially abuse similar fields if they are used by downstream systems.
Impact data integrity.

No privilege escalation or authorization bypass was observed.

Mitigation
Restrict modification of role-related fields to authorized administrative APIs only.
Ignore client-supplied role values.
Enforce server-side validation for immutable profile attributes.
Separate display metadata from authorization attributes.
Implement allowlists for updatable profile fields.
Ensure authorization decisions rely only on backend-controlled roles.
